    approach: [{"title":"Initial Assessment","body":"We begin by listening. A comprehensive conversation covers your current concerns, personal and family history, medical background, medications, sleep, substance use, and daily functioning. Screening questionnaires may be used to add structure and help track change over time.","iconPath":"M9 12h6m-6 4h6M7 4h10a2 2 0 012 2v14a2 2 0 01-2 2H7a2 2 0 01-2-2V6a2 2 0 012-2z"},{"title":"Personalized Plan","body":"Findings are explained in plain language. Together we discuss possible diagnoses, how they fit your experience, and the options available, which may include medication management, psychotherapy, lifestyle strategies, or referrals for further testing.","iconPath":"M9 5H7a2 2 0 00-2 2v12a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2V7a2 2 0 00-2-2h-2M9 5a2 2 0 002 2h2a2 2 0 002-2M9 5a2 2 0 012-2h2a2 2 0 012 2m-6 9l2 2 4-4"},{"title":"Ongoing Support","body":"An assessment is the start of a process. Follow-up visits allow us to review how you are doing, revisit the original impressions as new information appears, and adjust the plan so that care continues to fit your needs.","iconPath":"M4.318 6.318a4.5 4.5 0 000 6.364L12 20.364l7.682-7.682a4.5 4.5 0 00-6.364-6.364L12 7.636l-1.318-1.318a4.5 4.5 0 00-6.364 0z"}],
    benefits: [{"title":"Clarity","body":"A structured evaluation brings your story, symptoms, history, and goals together so scattered concerns make sense."},{"title":"Matched Treatment","body":"Accurate diagnosis and early identification lead to better matching of treatment to the individual."},{"title":"Plain-Language Findings","body":"Findings are explained in plain language, along with possible diagnoses and the options available."},{"title":"Ongoing Tracking","body":"Measuring symptoms over time helps guide care, and follow-up visits allow the plan to be adjusted."}],
    timelineHeading: "What to Expect",
    timeline: [{"title":"First Visit","body":"You will talk through your concerns, history, and goals. Expect detailed questions and possibly brief questionnaires. You may leave with initial impressions and next steps."},{"title":"Week 2-4","body":"Follow-up allows review of any additional information, such as records or lab results, and the start of agreed-upon treatment. Early changes may be small."},{"title":"Month 1-3","body":"We check progress, discuss side effects or challenges, and refine the plan. Many treatments take several weeks to show their full effect, so patience is part of the process."},{"title":"Long-term","body":"Care shifts toward maintaining progress and revisiting the assessment if symptoms or life circumstances change. Results vary from person to person, and no outcome can be guaranteed."}],
    extraSections: [{"heading":"Treatments that may follow an assessment","body":["What happens after an assessment depends on what we learn. Common options include medication management, where medicines such as antidepressants, anti-anxiety agents, mood stabilizers, or non-stimulant and stimulant treatments for attention difficulties are considered carefully and monitored for benefit and side effects.","Psychotherapy approaches, such as cognitive behavioral therapy, are often recommended alone or alongside medication. We may also discuss sleep habits, exercise, stress management, and support from family. When appropriate, we may suggest labs, medical follow-up, or specialized testing to rule out other contributors."]}],
    faqs: [{"q":"How long does a mental health assessment take?","a":"Length varies with the complexity of your concerns and the type of evaluation. A thorough assessment is typically more detailed than a routine follow-up visit. Call the office at (435) 200-3430 to ask about scheduling."},{"q":"Do I need to prepare anything?","a":"It helps to write down your symptoms, when they began, and what makes them better or worse. Bring a list of current medications and supplements, and any past records or prior diagnoses you have. Notes about sleep, mood, and stress are useful too."},{"q":"Will I receive a diagnosis?","a":"Often an assessment results in a diagnosis, but not always. Sometimes the outcome is a clearer picture of what is happening, a working impression to be monitored, or a recommendation for additional evaluation. Your clinician will explain what was found and what it means."},{"q":"Is what I share kept private?","a":"Mental health information is protected by health privacy laws such as HIPAA, and there are limited legal exceptions, such as imminent safety concerns. Ask about privacy practices at any time if you have questions."},{"q":"What if I am in crisis?","a":"An assessment appointment is not an emergency service. If you are in immediate danger or thinking about harming yourself, call 911 or dial 988 to reach the Suicide and Crisis Lifeline right away."}],
