    approach: [{"title":"Initial Assessment","body":"Treatment begins with an unhurried conversation about your symptoms, history, current medications, medical background, stressors and goals. This helps clarify what is going on and what kind of care is most likely to help.","iconPath":"M9 12h6m-6 4h6m2 5H7a2 2 0 01-2-2V5a2 2 0 012-2h5.586a1 1 0 01.707.293l5.414 5.414a1 1 0 01.293.707V19a2 2 0 01-2 2z"},{"title":"Personalized Plan","body":"No two people experience mental health conditions the same way. Your plan is built around your diagnosis, preferences and daily life, and may include medication, talk therapy, lifestyle strategies or a combination.","iconPath":"M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"},{"title":"Ongoing Support","body":"Follow-up visits allow progress to be reviewed, side effects to be discussed and the plan to be adjusted. Mental health care works best as a steady partnership rather than a one-time event.","iconPath":"M4.318 6.318a4.5 4.5 0 000 6.364L12 20.364l7.682-7.682a4.5 4.5 0 00-6.364-6.364L12 7.636l-1.318-1.318a4.5 4.5 0 00-6.364 0z"}],
    benefits: [{"title":"Accurate Diagnosis","body":"Treatment starts with an accurate diagnosis and a plan built around your symptoms, preferences and daily life."},{"title":"Combined Approaches","body":"Studies show that combining approaches often helps people who do not improve with a single one."},{"title":"Adjusted Over Time","body":"Treatment is adjusted over time based on how you respond."},{"title":"Steady Partnership","body":"Follow-up visits allow progress to be reviewed, side effects to be discussed and the plan to be adjusted."}],
    timelineHeading: "What to Expect",
    timeline: [{"title":"First Visit","body":"You share your story, ask questions and begin to understand your options. A diagnosis and initial plan may be discussed."},{"title":"Week 2-4","body":"Early follow-up checks how you are tolerating any medication and how therapy strategies are fitting into daily life. Some people notice small changes, while others need more time."},{"title":"Month 1-3","body":"Many treatments take several weeks to show their full effect. Doses or approaches may be adjusted as you and your provider learn what works best."},{"title":"Long-term","body":"Care shifts toward maintaining progress, preventing relapse and supporting your overall well-being. Visits are tailored to what you need."}],
    extraSections: [{"heading":"Treatments and therapies","body":["Depending on your needs, care may involve medication management, in which prescriptions are chosen carefully, explained clearly and monitored for benefit and side effects. It may also involve evidence-based talk therapy approaches such as cognitive behavioral therapy, supportive counseling or skills-based strategies for stress, sleep and emotional regulation.","Lifestyle factors also matter. Regular sleep, physical activity, nutrition, limiting alcohol and staying connected with others can all strengthen the effects of formal treatment. Your plan can include practical suggestions in these areas, always paced to what feels realistic for you."]},{"heading":"A Note on Recovery","body":["Recovery is rarely a straight line. Good days and harder days are normal, and your plan can change as you do."]}],
    faqs: [{"q":"What conditions can treatment help with?","a":"Mental health treatment is commonly used for depression, anxiety, ADHD, bipolar disorder, trauma-related conditions, obsessive-compulsive disorder and other concerns that affect mood, thinking and behavior. An assessment helps determine what applies to you."},{"q":"Will I need medication?","a":"Not necessarily. Some people benefit from medication, some from therapy, and many from a combination. The decision is made together, based on your symptoms, preferences and medical history."},{"q":"How long does it take for treatment to work?","a":"It varies. Some people feel better within a few weeks, while others need longer or several adjustments. Antidepressants, for example, often take several weeks to reach their full effect, so patience and regular follow-up matter."},{"q":"How do I know when to seek help?","a":"Consider reaching out when symptoms last more than a couple of weeks, interfere with work, relationships or sleep, or simply feel too heavy to manage alone. You do not need to wait for a crisis. If you are in immediate danger, call 911 or the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline."},{"q":"How do I get started at Blue Willow Psychiatry?","a":"Call (435) 200-3430 or use our contact page. We are located at 28 North Main Street in Tooele, UT 84074, and we can answer questions about next steps."}],
