    approach: [{"title":"Initial Assessment","body":"We begin by listening. A thorough review of your symptoms, history, current and past medications, medical conditions, and goals gives us the full picture before any decision is made.","iconPath":"M9 12h6m-6 4h6M9 8h6M7 3h10a2 2 0 012 2v14a2 2 0 01-2 2H7a2 2 0 01-2-2V5a2 2 0 012-2z"},{"title":"Personalized Plan","body":"Together we discuss options, expected benefits, and possible side effects. Your preferences matter, and the plan is built around your needs rather than a one-size-fits-all approach.","iconPath":"M12 6v6l4 2m6-2a10 10 0 11-20 0 10 10 0 0120 0z"},{"title":"Ongoing Support","body":"Follow-up visits track how you are responding. Doses can be adjusted, medications changed, or plans updated as your needs evolve over time.","iconPath":"M4.318 6.318a4.5 4.5 0 000 6.364L12 20.364l7.682-7.682a4.5 4.5 0 00-6.364-6.364L12 7.636l-1.318-1.318a4.5 4.5 0 00-6.364 0z"}],
    benefits: [{"title":"Collaborative Care","body":"Your preferences matter, and the plan is built around your needs rather than a one-size-fits-all approach."},{"title":"Careful Monitoring","body":"Careful monitoring helps catch side effects early and ensures treatment keeps pace with your life."},{"title":"Local, Consistent Follow-Up","body":"For people in Tooele and nearby communities, local, consistent care can make that follow-up easier to maintain."},{"title":"Coordinated Support","body":"We may discuss psychotherapy, sleep habits, physical activity, stress management, and coordination with other health professionals so your care fits together."}],
    timelineHeading: "What to Expect",
    timeline: [{"title":"First Visit","body":"A detailed conversation about your symptoms, history, and goals. If medication is appropriate, we talk through the options and decide on next steps together."},{"title":"Week 2-4","body":"A check-in on how you are tolerating the medication. Some people notice early changes, while many medications need several weeks to show their full effect."},{"title":"Month 1-3","body":"Fine-tuning. Doses may be adjusted, and we review benefits, side effects, and whether the plan fits your daily life."},{"title":"Long-term","body":"Regular monitoring keeps treatment on track. We periodically reassess whether your medication is still the right choice for you."}],
    extraSections: [{"heading":"Medications and supportive care","body":["Depending on your diagnosis, treatment may involve antidepressants, anti-anxiety medications, mood stabilizers, antipsychotics, or medications for attention and sleep. Each class works differently, and we explain what to expect, including common side effects and what to report.","Medication often works best alongside other supports. We may discuss psychotherapy, sleep habits, physical activity, stress management, and coordination with other health professionals so your care fits together."]},{"heading":"Timeline note","body":["Every person responds differently, and timelines are general guides rather than promises."]}],
    faqs: [{"q":"What is medication management?","a":"It is the ongoing process of prescribing, monitoring, and adjusting psychiatric medication to help relieve symptoms while keeping side effects as manageable as possible."},{"q":"How long will I need to take medication?","a":"It varies by person and condition. Some people take medication for a limited period, while others benefit from longer-term treatment. This is a decision we revisit together over time."},{"q":"How soon will I feel a difference?","a":"Timing depends on the medication. Some effects, like improved sleep, may appear sooner, while others such as antidepressant benefits commonly take several weeks. Patience and follow-up are important."},{"q":"Can I stop my medication if I feel better?","a":"Please talk with your prescriber first. Stopping suddenly can cause withdrawal effects or a return of symptoms, and a gradual, supervised plan is usually safer."},{"q":"Do you prescribe controlled substances?","a":"We can, and often will, prescribe controlled substances, but only on an individual basis and only if we agree that the benefit of the medication outweighs the risks. Controlled substances are not prescribed at your first visit, and you will need to be seen in office periodically if you receive them from us. We might ask for outside records or drug screens to ensure your safety and well-being. If, after a careful assessment, we do not agree with a long-standing medication, we can explore other options and, if necessary, create a taper plan."},{"q":"Is medication the only option?","a":"No. Medication is often combined with psychotherapy, lifestyle changes, and social support. We can discuss what mix makes sense for your situation."}],
